{
  "schema": 2,
  "purpose": "Retrieval coverage evaluation across all four advisory areas of the Agriculture domain: crop, livestock, weather and market. Measures what fraction of realistic farmer questions the corpus supports, and whether questions requiring live data are correctly refused rather than invented.",
  "notes": [
    "Questions use the register a farmer would actually use: symptom descriptions, not diagnostic terminology. A farmer says 'the leaves are yellow and twisted', not 'diagnose cassava mosaic virus'. Evaluating on textbook phrasing would flatter retrieval and tell us nothing about field use.",
    "THE CRITICAL DISTINCTION: 'can we advise on this topic' is not the same as 'can we know this fact'. We can advise on marketing without knowing today's price, and on planting timing without a forecast. Questions needing a live fact stay in the refusal set; questions needing judgement grounded in extension material are in scope.",
    "in_scope=false items are the refusal test, not gaps to fix. A system that answers 'what is maize selling for today?' from a 2011 document is worse than one that declines - it will be confidently, specifically wrong.",
    "area tags drive per-area coverage reporting, so a gap in livestock or market is visible rather than averaged away.",
    "expect_any: a retrieved passage must contain at least one of these terms to count as a hit. Added after discovering that coverage measured PRESENCE, not RELEVANCE - a question about cassava mosaic scored as covered when retrieval returned a passage about cassava scales. The earlier 88% figure was inflated."
  ],
